 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455409 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455409 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455409 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455409 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186455180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455409 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c62d441d-cf47-52e7-b38c-fb09c667ff01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455449 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455449 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455449 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455449 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=f(x),\\,y(2)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $3$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(2)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455449 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45c14947-ce9d-54ff-a45e-efd6339354f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455449 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(2)=6.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & =A{e}^{-4(2)}+{y}_{p}(2) \\\\ 6 & =A{e}^{-8}+0 \\\\ A & =6{e}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(6{e}^{8}){e}^{-4x}=6{e}^{8-4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼6{e}^{8-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455480 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455480 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455480 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455480 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,y(0)=-4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an even, $4$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455480 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96918677-d74f-5a11-bcee-c80ebc5a3b95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455480 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an even function and the differential operator contains no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits the symmetry and is also an even function consisting only of cosine terms. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+2=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{a}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x).\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{2}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we use the condition $y(0)=-4.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x).\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=-4$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =-4-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A$ back into ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(-4-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455496 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455496 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455496 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455496 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+3{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455496 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc163f49-3433-502e-a178-2f1ecb1f8055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455496 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+3{\\pi }^{2}=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\pi \\,\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{3}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B\\sqrt{3}\\pi +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B\\sqrt{3}\\pi  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{\\sqrt{3}{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x)$, we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
